<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Mema</h1><p class="st-meta">1000 CE – 1450 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Soninke kingdom on the western edge of the inland Niger delta, a successor to the Ghana Empire that sheltered the young Sundiata Keita.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mema">Mema</a> lay west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-debo">Lake Debo</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>, a region of lakes and pasture once far wetter than today. It was a province of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ghana-empire">Ghana Empire</a> and emerged as an independent kingdom as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a> declined after the 11th century. Archaeological survey around Akumbu shows a dense cluster of settlements and iron-smelting sites.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Little is recorded of its government. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sundiata-keita">Sundiata</a> epic, the exiled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mande-peoples">Mande</a> prince found refuge with the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mema">Mema</a>, who gave him horsemen for his campaign against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sosso-kingdom">Sosso kingdom</a>. Mema's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soninke">Soninke</a> elite traded along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> and kept horses, a source of military strength.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mema">Mema</a> became a province of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a> in the 13th century and was later absorbed by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/songhai-empire">Songhai Empire</a>, around the middle of the 15th century. A drying climate drove much of its population away, and the region is now sparsely inhabited.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1000 CE – 1450 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">West Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:79.60%;width:8.96%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1000 CE – 1450 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Soninke traditional religion and Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Soninke</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mali">Mali</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mema, Meema</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1225&amp;focus=kingdom-of-mema" aria-label="Kingdom of Mema on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4631 1492 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Mema lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4782 1851h0M4769 1989h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4951 1748h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M4951 1748h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Mali. <a href="/atlas?year=1225&amp;focus=kingdom-of-mema">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

